---
description: Prefer the control that finishes an action in the fewest taps
alwaysApply: true
---

# Fewer taps

Screens are used on a phone. If two controls can do the same job, use the one that takes fewer taps.

## Choices

- A dropdown (`select`) takes two taps: open it, then pick. For a short fixed list (2–4 options), use `ChoiceField` so one tap selects it.
- Keep a dropdown only when the list is long enough that buttons would make people scroll (countries, many groups).
- A yes/no or on/off choice is a tap on the option itself, not a dropdown.

```tsx
// Bad — two taps for two options
<Field label="Role" required>
  <select name="role" defaultValue="leader">
    <option value="leader">Leader</option>
    <option value="asst-leader">Asst. Leader</option>
  </select>
</Field>

// Good — one tap
<ChoiceField
  label="Role"
  name="role"
  required
  options={GROUP_ROLE_OPTIONS}
  defaultValue="leader"
/>
```

## Extra steps

- Do not add a menu, confirm screen, or second page when one control on the current screen can finish the action.
- Do not make people open something, then choose, when the choices fit on the screen.
